course: dictionary
lesson: 12365
title: Many meanings: crook
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings. To find the right one, read the words around it: they are its context. «The crook was caught by the police after the bank robbery.» Crooks are dishonest people, and the police and the robbery are the clues, so a crook means a criminal here.
explain: «The shepherd leaned on his crook while the sheep grazed.» A shepherd is a person who looks after sheep. Would a shepherd lean on a dishonest person? No. So here a crook is a long stick with a hook at the top.
explain: «She crooked her finger to call me over.» Here crook is a verb, a doing word. She bent her finger into a hook shape. Always try your meaning in the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: A crook can mean a dishonest person, such as a thief or a cheat.
fact: «Crook» can also mean a shepherd's long stick with a hook at the top.
fact: «The crook of your arm» is the inside part where your arm bends.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, «crook» informally means ill or unwell: «He's crook, he's staying home.»
fact: «By hook or by crook» means by any means at all.
exercise: «The crook was caught by the police.» What is a crook in this sentence? | *A dishonest person | A type of fish | The bend of your arm | A shepherd's stick
exercise: «She crooked her finger to call me over.» What does crooked mean here? | To wave goodbye | To break a finger | *To bend a finger into a hook shape | To point up at the sky
exercise: «I'm feeling crook, so I'm staying in bed.» In Australia or New Zealand, what does crook mean? | Bent like a hook | Cold and wet | *Ill or unwell | Very happy and full of energy
test: «The farmer leaned on his crook and watched his sheep.» What is the crook in this sentence? | *A long stick with a hook at the top | A broken toy | The inside of a bent arm | A dishonest person
test: «He held the teddy close in the crook of his arm.» What does the crook of his arm mean? | *The inside part where the arm bends | A long wooden stick | A dishonest thief | A bent old tree
exam: «By hook or by crook, Sam was going to finish the race.» What does by hook or by crook mean? | *By any means at all | Very slowly and quietly | With a long stick and a hook | Only by running up the hill
